<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/the-american-civil-war">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cambridge’s key question 4 asks <strong>why there was a civil war in the United States and
    what its results were</strong> — and its focus points hand you the essay in four moves: how
    far did slavery cause it, why did Lincoln’s election matter, why did the North win, and did
    the war change anything? The short version: <strong>a nation half slave and half free could
    keep the peace only while there was unclaimed land to argue about</strong>. When the
    territories ran out, so did the compromises.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from compromise to catastrophe, 1820–77 — filter by phase with the chips" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <h2>The engine of the quarrel: slavery</h2>
  <p>
    After Eli Whitney’s cotton gin (1793), cotton — grown by enslaved labour — became the
    South’s whole economy, worth over half of US export earnings by 1860 and demanding
    constant new land. The North, industrialising on free labour, grew morally opposed as
    abolitionists (Garrison, former slaves like Frederick Douglass, Harriet Beecher Stowe’s
    <em>Uncle Tom’s Cabin</em>, 1852) made slavery a national conscience issue. Each
    compromise managed the quarrel and each collapsed: Missouri (1820) drew a line; the
    Compromise of 1850 admitted California free but enforced slave-catching in Boston;
    Kansas-Nebraska (1854) let settlers vote and produced small-scale civil war; and the Dred
    Scott ruling (1857) declared that Congress had never been allowed to ban slavery anywhere.
    By 1858 Lincoln could say the nation could not endure “half slave and half free”; by 1859,
    John Brown had tried to start the uprising himself.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the enslaved population of the United States at each census, 1790–1860" widget="plotter" config={censusCfg} />

  <p>
    The curve is the stakes. From just under 700,000 in 1790 to nearly four million by 1860 —
    growing by around 30 per cent every decade, almost entirely through natural increase —
    it made slavery the region’s labour system, property regime and social order at once. The
    political fights of 1820–57 were arguments about this curve’s future: <em>where could it
    legally grow?</em> Lincoln’s election in 1860 meant, for the first time, a president whose
    party existed to say “nowhere”. The South answered with secession before he even took
    office; the war began at Fort Sumter on 12 April 1861.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the North won</h2>
  <p>
    The South only had to survive; the North had to conquer — but the numbers were merciless.
    The Union held about 22 million people against the Confederacy’s 9 million (nearly 4
    million of them enslaved, and watched); it owned the overwhelming majority of the
    factories, over two-thirds of the railway mileage and virtually the whole navy, whose
    blockade slowly strangled cotton exports and imports of war goods. Turning that weight
    into victory took time and blood: <strong>July 1863 was the hinge</strong> — Gettysburg
    (1–3 July, about 51,000 casualties) ended Lee’s second invasion of the North, and
    Vicksburg’s fall on 4 July cut the Confederacy in half along the Mississippi. The
    <strong>Emancipation Proclamation</strong> (1 January 1863) redefined the war against
    slavery, brought roughly 180,000 Black soldiers into Union blue, and made British or
    French intervention on the Confederacy’s behalf politically impossible. Grant’s attrition
    and Sherman’s march through Georgia then broke the South’s capacity to resist; Lee
    surrendered at Appomattox on 9 April 1865, and Lincoln was assassinated five days later.
  </p>

  <h2>Did the war change anything?</h2>
  <p>
    On paper, everything: the <strong>13th Amendment</strong> (1865) abolished slavery, the
    <strong>14th</strong> (1868) made citizenship and equal protection constitutional, the
    <strong>15th</strong> (1870) banned racial bars on voting — a “second founding” of the
    Republic. Reconstruction briefly made it real: Black voters, congressmen, public schools,
    federal troops protecting the franchise. But white supremacist violence (the Ku Klux
    Klan), economic coercion into sharecropping, and northern fatigue dismantled it, and the
    Compromise of 1877 withdrew the last federal protection. So the exam verdict holds both
    halves: the war changed the Constitution absolutely and society far too little — leaving
    the unfinished business that the civil-rights movement of the 1950s and 1960s would take
    up, a century later.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE History 0470 syllabus (2027–2028), Core Option A key question 4; McPherson, Battle Cry of Freedom; Foner, Reconstruction: America’s Unfinished Revolution.</p>
